00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.610 align:start position:0%
for over 30 years scientists have tried  and failed to design a drug that tackles  the cause not the symptoms of  Alzheimer's disease now comes the first  evidence of real success an antibody  infusion that attacks a specific protein  in the brain slowing the development of  early stage Alzheimer's by around a  quarter  professor John Hardy first developed the  theory we said that this approach might  work 30 years ago and you know if you'd  asked me 30 years ago how long it would  take I'd say five to ten years so for me  it's it seems definitely momentous I  think for patients it's a real step  forward a real step forward and  um it's going to take a couple of years  to get it into the system  it's patients like John teeling in the  early stages of the disease who stand to  benefit the most John says he felt the  ground open up when he was diagnosed  he'd Embrace anything that gives him  more time with his wife and family you  put it out of your mind you know what  you're going to end up like and you know  there's nothing you can do about it but  you can't worry about it because there's  nothing you can do  try and be accepting of it the new drug  lecanumab inhibits the buildup in the  brain of a protein called beta amyloid  visible as so-called plaques in the  brains of Alzheimer's patients nearly  1800 patients around the world took part  in the study which showed a 27 slowdown  in the deterioration of their cognitive  skills over 18 months but if approved  the treatment will be laborious and  costly to administer through blood  infusions twice a month and will also  require major investment in screening  for the disease to catch it sufficiently  early and at the moment we do that  through pet scans or through cerebral  spinal fluid testing through from a  lumbar puncture  um and that is uh we need to broaden  access to ways in which we can have  access to those kind of tests we also  want to see new techniques that are  quicker and faster and potentially  cheaper for example blood tests 30  million people around the world suffer  with Alzheimer's in itself just one form  of dementia much difficult and costly  work remains before treatments let alone  cures are widely available but this is a  hugely significant First Step Harry  Fawcett Al Jazeera London
 

